        "contenuto_esteso": "To display this content from YouTube, you must enable advertisement tracking and audience measurement. One of your browser extensions seems to be blocking the video player from loading. To watch this content, you may need to disable it on this site. 'Pakistani military calling the shots: Has connections with IRCG, very good relationship with Trump' Middle East Issued on: 08/04/2026 - 23:15 Modified: 08/04/2026 - 23:34 Nadia Massih is pleased to welcome Ali Vaez, Director of Iran Project & Senior Advisor at the International Crisis Group. While the US sees the outcome as a decisive military victory, Vaez argues that the more consequential story lies in the reconfiguration of power within Iran. From Tehran’s perspective, survival in the face of overwhelming force constitutes a form of victory. At the same time, this war has not left Iran unchanged. Power has tilted toward more hardline actors, particularly the Revolutionary Guard, reshaping the internal hierarchy of the Islamic Republic. US-Iran truce: ‘Sensitive behind-the-scenes conversations’ paved way for Pakistan-brokered ceasefire 08/04/2026 Middle East Trump administration's claims of victory in Iran 'really quite hollow', analyst says 08/04/2026 Middle East Netanyahu says Israel ready to 'return to battle at any moment' against Iran 08/04/2026 Middle East Iran continues to target Gulf countries despite ceasefire announcement 08/04/2026 Middle East Iran closes Strait of Hormuz again as Israel pounds Lebanon, air defense systems go off in Tehran 08/04/2026 Middle East Israel's attacks on Beirut 'much bigger than anything we've seen in this war or the previous one' 08/04/2026 Middle East 'Unlawful' war on Iran: 'Use of force by the United States never justified' 08/04/2026 Middle East US war on Iran: Lucas makes case for 'discussions & dialogue over renewing violence & confrontation' 08/04/2026 Middle East US-Iran ceasefire ‘a major problem domestically’ for Netanyahu 08/04/2026 Middle East"
